<body>
<div class="Section1">

<div class="cover-page">
    <div class="cover-title">PROJECT MARG</div>
    <div class="cover-subtitle">Multi-criteria Alignment & Greenfield Routing Engine for Highway Corridors</div>
    <div class="cover-tagline">An Automated Multi-Criteria Decision-Support System for Greenfield Highway and Controlled-Access Expressway Planning</div>
    <hr style="width: 60%; border: 1pt solid #1f4e78; margin: 25pt auto;">
    <div class="cover-meta">
        <p><b>Comprehensive System Documentation & Technical Blueprint</b></p>
        <p><b>Target Applications:</b> DPR Feasibility Automation &bull; PM Gati Shakti Master Planning &bull; Bharatmala Pariyojana</p>
        <p><b>Technology Stack:</b> Java 25 &bull; Spring Boot 3.5 &bull; React 18 &bull; Leaflet.js &bull; JTS Topology Suite &bull; ISRO Bhuvan GIS</p>
        <p><b>Core Algorithms:</b> Metric A* Search with Octile Heuristic &bull; Lagrangian Relaxation &bull; Post-Mainline Network Planner</p>
    </div>
</div>

<h1>1. Executive Summary: What the Model Is About</h1>
<p><b>Project MARG</b> (<b>M</b>ulti-criteria <b>A</b>lignment and <b>G</b>reenfield <b>R</b>outing Engine) is an autonomous, high-performance geospatial optimization platform engineered to revolutionize linear transportation infrastructure planning—specifically greenfield highways and access-controlled expressways.</p>
<p>In civil engineering terminology, a <b>greenfield alignment</b> refers to an entirely new transportation corridor planned through unpaved natural terrain (forests, agricultural basins, river valleys, and rural tracts) where no prior road exists, in contrast to brownfield widening of existing routes.</p>
<p>MARG automates the generation of <b>Pareto-optimal highway planning corridors</b> between any two geographic coordinates across India. Instead of treating highway design as a single-objective problem (such as minimizing Euclidean distance or earthwork cut-and-fill volume), MARG establishes a continuous, projected metric search surface that concurrently balances four engineering pillars:</p>
<ul>
    <li><b>Environmental Conservation:</b> Active avoidance of national parks, tiger reserves, elephant migration paths, and riparian wetlands.</li>
    <li><b>Social & Demographic Safeguards:</b> Minimization of residential displacement, prevention of village bisection, and avoidance of sacred heritage structures.</li>
    <li><b>Engineering Feasibility & Safety:</b> Adherence to Indian Roads Congress (<b>IRC</b>) geometric design standards, avoidance of steep mountain scarps (&gt; 30&deg;), and prevention of seismic fault crossings.</li>
    <li><b>Economic Directness & Regional Connectivity:</b> Enforcing strict corridor length budgets, placing grade-separated interchanges, and building secondary feeder spur roads to connect intermediate market hubs (<i>mandis</i>) and <i>tehsils</i>.</li>
</ul>

<h1>2. The Need: Why MARG Was Built</h1>
<p>Linear transportation infrastructure forms the backbone of India's economic growth under flagship initiatives like the <b>Bharatmala Pariyojana</b> and the <b>PM Gati Shakti National Master Plan</b>. However, the preparation of Feasibility Studies and Detailed Project Reports (DPRs) by agencies such as the National Highways Authority of India (NHAI) and the Ministry of Road Transport and Highways (MoRTH) suffers from four critical systemic deficiencies:</p>
<ol>
    <li><b>Siloed, Sequential Domain Analysis:</b> Alignment design, Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA), Social Impact Assessment (SIA), and geotechnical risk evaluation are traditionally performed sequentially. An alignment optimized purely for geometric speed often traverses reserved forests, elephant migration corridors, or prime agricultural land, leading to multi-year litigation, public protests, and severe cost overruns.</li>
    <li><b>Protracted Statutory Clearance Delays:</b> Approvals under the <b>Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980</b> and wildlife clearances from the <b>Standing Committee of the National Board for Wildlife (SC-NBWL)</b> under the <b>Wildlife (Protection) Act, 1972</b> frequently stall projects for months or years during the tendering phase because ecological constraints were not integrated into initial route generation.</li>
    <li><b>The "Sealed Pipe" Connectivity Deficit:</b> Mainline expressways are often designed as high-speed "pipes" connecting distant metropolises, bypassing intermediate rural growth centers, agricultural <i>mandis</i>, and sub-district towns. Retrofitting secondary feeder connections post-construction leads to chaotic, substandard traffic distribution.</li>
    <li><b>Prolonged Planning Cycles:</b> Conventional DPR corridor alignment alternatives require <b>3 to 6 months</b> of manual desk-based GIS tracing and iterative field surveys.</li>
</ol>
<p><b>MARG solves these challenges</b> by synthesizing multi-criteria evaluation, regulatory pre-auditing, structural classification, and network planning into a single algorithmic pipeline executing in <b>under 2 seconds</b>.</p>

<h1>3. Literature Survey & How MARG Overcomes Shortcomings</h1>
<p class="table-caption">Table 3.1: Comparative Analysis of Highway Alignment Paradigms</p>
<table>
    <tr>
        <th style="width: 18%;">Era / Paradigm</th>
        <th style="width: 20%;">Representative Systems</th>
        <th style="width: 20%;">Methodology & Data</th>
        <th style="width: 22%;">Key Limitations</th>
        <th style="width: 20%;">How MARG Overcomes Them</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>1980s–1990s: Geometric & Dynamic Programming</b></td>
        <td>Chew et al. (1989); Nicholson (1973)</td>
        <td>Dynamic Programming on coarse Digital Terrain Models (DTMs).</td>
        <td>Focused strictly on 2D/simplified earthwork cut-and-fill. Zero integration with polygonal land use, ecology, or demographic datasets.</td>
        <td>MARG synthesizes a <b>13-layer concurrent cost surface</b> combining satellite LULC, flood risk, protected areas, demographics, and building footprints.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>2000s–2010s: Genetic Algorithms (GAs)</b></td>
        <td>Jong & Schonfeld (2003); Jha & Schonfeld (2004); Kang et al. (2012)</td>
        <td>Multi-Objective Genetic Algorithms (NSGA-II) using B-spline representations.</td>
        <td>Stochastic non-reproducibility; slow convergence on regional extents (&gt; 50 km); severe computational overhead; cannot handle acute crossing angles.</td>
        <td>MARG uses an exact, deterministic <b>Metric A* graph search with Lagrangian Relaxation</b>, delivering reproducible Pareto-optimal corridors across 400+ km in seconds.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>Commercial Highway Design Software</b></td>
        <td>Trimble Quantm; Bentley OpenRoads</td>
        <td>Proprietary corridor optimization engines using high-res CAD and user cost bands.</td>
        <td>"Black-box" proprietary weighting; prohibitive licensing costs; zero integration with Indian statutory clearance laws or IRC specifications.</td>
        <td>MARG is open, transparent, adheres strictly to <b>IRC:73, IRC:SP:84, and IRC:SP:87</b>, and automatically audits compliance with the Wildlife Protection Act, 1972.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>Modern GIS-MCDA & Raster Pathfinding</b></td>
        <td>Pushkarev et al. (2018); Effat & Hassan (2013)</td>
        <td>Least Cost Path (LCP) and Dijkstra on unprojected raster impedance grids.</td>
        <td>Angular degree distortion (&gt; 15%); unconstrained serpentine detours; lack of crossing skew penalties; no post-mainline network planning.</td>
        <td>MARG resolves all 5 core research gaps detailed below.</td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h3>The 5 Core Research Gaps Resolved by MARG:</h3>
<ol>
    <li><b>Elimination of Geographic Degree Distortion:</b> Prior academic models calculate distances on unprojected latitude/longitude grids (&Delta;lat, &Delta;lon). At Indian latitudes (8&deg;N to 36&deg;N), 1&deg; of longitude contracts from 110.57 km in southern Tamil Nadu to 89.83 km in Kashmir. Degree-based searches heavily distort path geometry by under-penalizing East-West movements. MARG projects all spatial layers into local <b>Universal Transverse Mercator (UTM Zones 42N–47N)</b> metric planes, achieving sub-millimetre forward/inverse reversibility.</li>
    <li><b>Strict Route Elongation Bounds via Lagrangian Relaxation:</b> Standard Least-Cost Path (LCP) algorithms produce serpentine alignments that wind excessively around low-cost patches. MARG enforces a hard physical length ceiling: <code>L(route) &le; L_baseline &times; (1 + &delta;_max / 100)</code> using dual-stage Lagrangian Relaxation.</li>
    <li><b>Skew-Angle Crossing Cost Formulation:</b> Civil engineering costs for bridges and overpasses depend on the angle of intersection. Crossing a 200 m river or active railway line at a 30&deg; skew requires a bridge span approximately double that of an orthogonal (90&deg;) crossing. MARG incorporates directional vector dot products to penalize acute crossings and parallel runs along river banks and fault scarps.</li>
    <li><b>Post-Mainline Network Engineering:</b> Existing systems terminate at the mainline centerline. MARG automatically places IRC-compliant grade-separated interchanges (&ge; 15 km spacing; ground slope &le; 12&deg;) and routes secondary A* feeder spurs to connect nearby economic centers and <i>mandis</i>.</li>
    <li><b>Automated Statutory Clearance Audit & 2D RoW Footprint Analysis:</b> Instead of a 1D line, MARG expands the alignment into a <b>2D Right-of-Way polygon</b> (60 m direct acquisition, 100 m construction buffer, 250 m environmental buffer) and pre-audits the corridor against protected wildlife boundaries to generate statutory clearance schedules.</li>
</ol>

<h1>4. The 13 Layers of the Spatial Model</h1>
<p>MARG discretizes the geographic search space into an artificial metric graph with default ground resolution &Delta;s = 250 m. The impedance cost of traversing an edge between nodes u and v is formulated as:</p>
<div class="formula-box">
Cost(u, v) = d(u, v) &times; [ 1.0 + &Sigma; ( w_k &times; p_k(v) ) + &Omega;_crossing(u, v) ]
</div>
<p>Where <code>d(u, v)</code> is the physical Euclidean distance (250 m for orthogonal moves, 353.55 m for diagonal moves), <code>p_k(v) &ge; 0</code> is the dimensionless penalty contribution of the k-th spatial layer at node v, <code>w_k</code> is the request-scoped user preference multiplier, and <code>&Omega;_crossing</code> is the geometric crossing penalty.</p>

<p class="table-caption">Table 4.1: Comprehensive Specification of the 13 Spatial Cost Layers</p>
<table>
    <tr>
        <th style="width: 5%;">#</th>
        <th style="width: 18%;">Layer Name</th>
        <th style="width: 22%;">Primary Data Source</th>
        <th style="width: 32%;">Penalty Values & Classification Bands</th>
        <th style="width: 23%;">Civil & Statutory Engineering Purpose</th>
    </tr>
