<h1>11. Plain-Language Guide to Map Controls, Icons &amp; Visuals</h1>
<p>This section explains key interactive controls, impact calculations, status badges, and visual map lines seen in the MARG web interface in simple, non-technical terms.</p>

<h2>11.1 What is "Detailed Live Map Scan"?</h2>
<p>MARG comes with three pre-loaded benchmark regions (Assam, Western Ghats, and Gangetic Plains) where detailed 13-layer maps are already saved on the server. When a user picks a <b>custom start and end point anywhere else in India</b>, MARG normally runs a fast lightweight scan so that a route is returned in 1–2 seconds.</p>
<p>Turning <b>Detailed Live Map Scan ON</b> tells the system to run a full on-demand data download and raster-building pipeline (<code>scripts/ingest_corridor.py</code>) specifically for that custom region—gathering detailed terrain slopes, forests, flood zones, buildings, rivers, and demographic layers before routing. It takes a bit longer on the first run (up to ~90 seconds), but delivers full 13-layer precision for custom locations.</p>

<h2>11.2 What is the "Mainline RoW Corridor" Button inside the LULC Layer Detail?</h2>
<p><b>RoW</b> stands for <b>Right-of-Way</b>—the actual physical strip of land that must be acquired and cleared to construct the highway (<b>60 meters wide</b> for an Expressway or <b>45 meters wide</b> for a National Highway, including lanes, shoulders, drainage, and safety buffers).</p>
<p>Inside the LULC (Land &amp; Forest Cover) layer detail card, the <b>Mainline RoW Corridor</b> button appears under the <i>"Shown on Map — click to zoom in"</i> list. It acts as a quick <b>zoom-to-location shortcut</b> that moves the map directly onto the highway's land-acquisition strip so you can visually inspect what forest, farmland, or open land falls inside the road footprint.</p>

<h2>11.3 How Are Affected Buildings, Families, and People Calculated?</h2>
<p><i>Example statement: "Only 2 buildings are affected, resulting in 5 families with a total of 24 people needing relocation."</i></p>
<p><b>Layers Involved:</b></p>
<ul>
    <li><b>Layer 11 (Building Footprints &amp; Demolition):</b> Uses real building outlines from OpenStreetMap to find every structure that physically overlaps the highway's Direct Right-of-Way (60 m / 45 m) strip.</li>
    <li><b>Layer 12 (Demographic Density &amp; Social Vulnerability) &amp; 2D RoW Corridor Engine:</b> Applies Indian land-acquisition and resettlement norms under the <b>RFCTLARR Act, 2013</b>.</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Step-by-Step Calculation:</b></p>
<ol>
    <li><b>Buildings Affected (2):</b> MARG overlays the 60 m / 45 m 2D road strip onto the building footprints map and counts how many buildings sit directly inside the acquisition boundary (here, 2 buildings).</li>
    <li><b>Families / Households (5):</b> For each affected building, MARG checks the building type (residential, commercial, etc.) and total floor area (<code>footprint area &times; number of floors</code>), then divides by <b>75 sq. meters per household</b> (standard RFCTLARR housing norm), with at least 1 household per residential floor. Because multi-story or larger buildings can house 2–3 families each, 2 buildings can correspond to <b>5 families</b>.</li>
    <li><b>Total People Needing Relocation (24):</b> MARG multiplies the estimated families by <b>4.8 persons per household</b> (India's standard RFCTLARR average household size): <code>5 families &times; 4.8 = 24 people</code>.</li>
</ol>

<h2>11.4 Meaning of Status Badges &amp; Icons (OK, REVIEW, 8x, -60.21%, SAFE)</h2>
<p>Next to each of the 13 layers in the right-hand panel, MARG displays a quick status chip showing how well the planned route performed compared to a straight-line path:</p>
<p class="table-caption">Table 11.1: Plain-Language Guide to Layer Status Badges</p>
<table>
    <tr>
        <th style="width: 18%;">Badge / Icon</th>
        <th style="width: 24%;">Meaning</th>
        <th style="width: 58%;">Simple Explanation</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>SAFE</b> (Green)</td>
        <td>Hard Safety Rule Passed</td>
        <td>A strict "must-not-break" legal or safety rule was 100% satisfied (for example, 0 National Parks crossed or no cliffs steeper than 30&deg;).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>-60.21%</b> (Blue/Teal)</td>
        <td>Verified Risk Reduction</td>
        <td>Compared to a simple straight-line road, MARG's smart route reduced harm on this layer by that percentage (e.g., <b>-60.21%</b> means 60.21% less forest, flood risk, or settlement impact).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>8x</b> (or 3x, 5x)</td>
        <td>Protected by Design</td>
        <td>The road had to cross this obstacle (such as rivers, railways, or wildlife corridors), so MARG automatically added that many protective structures (e.g., <b>8x</b> = 8 bridges, flyovers, or wildlife crossings).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>REVIEW</b> (Amber)</td>
        <td>Needs Engineer Review</td>
        <td>Something important requires human review before final approval (e.g., a few buildings fall in the path, a wetland is close by, or an eco-sensitive zone buffer requires clearance).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>OK</b> (Gray)</td>
        <td>Checked &amp; Clear</td>
        <td>The layer was checked with no major conflicts, no hard-rule violations, and no special extra structures needed.</td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2>11.5 What Are the Extra Thin Lines Visible in Layers Like LULC and Settlements?</h2>
<p>When you switch on a color-coded map layer (such as <b>Land &amp; Forest Cover [LULC]</b> or <b>Towns &amp; Villages [Settlements]</b>), you may notice <b>two lines close together at sharp bends</b>: a <b>thick bright colored line</b> (yellow/orange/green with a dark border) and a <b>thin faint line</b> peeking slightly out to the side.</p>
<ul>
    <li><b>The Thin Faint Line Underneath</b> is the <b>exact, full-detail Mainline Highway path</b> calculated by the A* router, which turns at every <b>250-meter</b> grid corner. When a color layer is active, the UI dims this line (semi-transparent) so the color coding stands out clearly.</li>
    <li><b>The Thick Colored Line on Top</b> is the <b>Color-Coded Layer Ribbon</b>, which paints each stretch according to the ground type underneath (for example, yellow for farmland/village outskirts, orange for close to homes). To keep the web map fast and smooth, this color ribbon samples points every <b>400 meters or more</b> along the route and connects them with straight segments.</li>
    <li><b>Why they separate slightly at corners:</b> Because the thick color ribbon jumps between ~400 m sample points while the thin real highway underneath bends at every 250 m grid point, the thick colored line slightly "cuts the corner" on sharp turns—revealing the thin actual route line underneath.</li>
</ul>

